/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ee3364-b57a-50d3-b9c1-1e44c4a66fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643332} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d1455d-1334-56bb-ae93-1ea034bfd963" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4a2491f-cde1-5b66-bf41-f5ac5ec2940b" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab6a640488e5b20fa205260aeb31fbcf6c49428087b2a62277a9e58118f3e850.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d65ba7-2d47-58e9-98df-d1268e0787d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643334} :answer-field/id #uuid "a574c658-09ca-56e2-a090-d347e8187c31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc3d00aa-46fe-581e-ac5c-06686ed74c14" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f2a5176bc5674bf5d755e0659aac28d7a2f9d2c7bf6af08c49a3f38658a6364.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6e07fa-871c-572f-9953-675d2d30b6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2141d16d-dd50-5459-9dfc-6d140670aef4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b81b87-fe8c-5604-9af6-783282a55dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643465} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2414124-5688-5fda-860d-53d2990e2917" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "002152e8-5b3c-5d0e-80a6-c79a1d4dfd75" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{51}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=51$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=51,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{51}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{51},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{51}=2\\sqrt{51},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bf4454-fc58-5647-9832-9af090145b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{94}$ and $2\\sqrt{83}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643457} {:answer/id #uuid "244c3a91-3a19-51ab-b47b-25a2b93ae66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{22}$ and $4\\sqrt{14}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643460} {:answer/id #uuid "bc933607-05e2-5404-a7f2-c2b4df14089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{21}$ and $4\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643470} {:answer/id #uuid "cb55091f-ee35-53a9-992c-cc2ae6250ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{19}$ and $2\\sqrt{57}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643474} {:answer/id #uuid "d53def7f-7dc6-56ed-a5a9-760c3d67e1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{10}$ and $8\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643460} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4b2297-162c-525a-aef6-b5874f51035c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1569191d-f868-5104-8548-6bb62bc99b93" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{88}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{56}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=88$ and ${b}^{2}=56.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=88,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{88}=2\\sqrt{22}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=56,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{56}=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{22},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{22}=4\\sqrt{22},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{14}=4\\sqrt{14}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb95e24-eabc-57f9-84e7-c15fd5f1b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643469} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf5848e1-7c80-5bbd-b0f1-ac9a7532aebc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232bd3e-4933-570c-a5fe-4788a09875f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643455} :answer-field/id #uuid "df7ff49a-ce3d-5e5e-9f7a-473613492fa1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f0bfeea-fcdf-5d3e-9275-beec38209c9a" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=30$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=30,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{30}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{30},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{30}=2\\sqrt{30},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818dea69-4bfb-56b5-aa32-71c3f92ded37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643464} {:answer/id #uuid "c41cc4f0-dc39-5198-8966-4b7bb9f16198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643472} {:answer/id #uuid "d380967b-4ac8-5a06-a099-b6f7e56cd31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643476} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50e36b-9a3b-5415-bc87-5b35b6b5516e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$16$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643480} {:answer/id #uuid "e770468c-74e3-5b59-a382-274ede177b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643483} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f5a0f0-17d0-5275-a79e-b64dd7eded9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8ff0d51-8878-558a-b310-4862399de7ad" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=7.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aa1992-3ffe-5540-b1ec-3f749521ec71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{97}$ and $4\\sqrt{17}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643458} {:answer/id #uuid "28a1b703-d1f1-5f25-a3bb-70baff366a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{79}$ and $6\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643461} {:answer/id #uuid "6a236c0c-47c5-56b0-818d-9dc761ef4b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{61}$ and $2\\sqrt{33}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643463} {:answer/id #uuid "c49d426c-d57a-598c-80f7-ad6cafd3180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{11}$ and $2\\sqrt{35}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643473} {:answer/id #uuid "cce169c9-efd6-5518-aef9-184e65503038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{85}$ and $2\\sqrt{78}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c534e9e-9262-5691-af1c-40c3f470e0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fcf6df82-9cf5-5ccd-974d-0b60b763b971" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{33}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{61}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=33$ and ${b}^{2}=61.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=33,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=61,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{61}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{61},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{61}=2\\sqrt{61},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{33}=2\\sqrt{33}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec222830-a6da-55ff-8d5d-ac2415d051ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645552} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce751b5-a34d-55ba-b400-d63917986adf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a5e9e1b-6d90-5170-858f-c7c55ff5ce37" :question/math-academy-id "q-316349" :question/problem "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-9,-1,4⟩$ and $b=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -9 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))i-((-9)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)j+((-9)\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =8i+24j+24k \\\\  & =⟨8,24,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{24}^{2}+{24}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+576+576} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1216} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f34f88a-e04a-5386-9347-411b050ccc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645238} {:answer/id #uuid "59da9c58-7614-51a8-ba0b-e31ea81d6e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{185}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645293} {:answer/id #uuid "7b62c949-2049-5c5c-b37f-5aa988e5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cdc4d7-fa2e-58ee-b378-62adc8135f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645429} {:answer/id #uuid "e35cc40d-377e-5405-81b9-ef9ed81cd00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{53}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645293} :answer-field/id #uuid "518f3ad4-b453-57c8-a0c1-a38fddb98ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c2ad278-6906-55f0-84d8-41e103e0abff" :question/math-academy-id "q-127904" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(9,4,-3),$ $B(4,4,-2),$ $C(5,6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+4j-2k)-(9i+4j-3k) \\\\  & =-5i+k \\\\  & =⟨-5,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+6j-4k)-(9i+4j-3k) \\\\  & =-4i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)i-((-5)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 1)j+((-5)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i-9j-10k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-10)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{185}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{185}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145ab775-da4b-5cbd-a7f6-1ed1d5b5ff2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645174} :answer-field/id #uuid "003d6d2b-866c-5f45-91be-cddef88fd39f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87f70111-bd9e-5384-9a7d-8f0f5f055592" :question/math-academy-id "q-316532" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(1,2,0),$ $B(4,3,1),$ $C(2,5,3).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =3i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+5j+3k)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b079e0df-9904-5c0a-9334-cc6c5372661a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{186}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645442}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645442} :answer-field/id #uuid "0deb096d-aca9-54d0-9162-922805e24584" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0c521b6-7c14-5b51-bd0f-0e13174aa5b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316396" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $BM$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(4,0,1)$, $B(0,3,-1)$, $C(-1,-1,2).$\n\n$BM=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $BM$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $BM,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AB}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i-j+2k)-(4i+k) \\\\  & =-5i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3j-k)-(4i+k) \\\\  & =-4i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3)i-((-5)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-4))j+((-5)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-4))k \\\\  & =1i+14j+19k \\\\  & =⟨1,14,19⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{19}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{558}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AC$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot AC \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,BM\\cdot \\sqrt{27} \\\\ \\sqrt{558} & =BM\\cdot \\sqrt{27} \\\\ BM & =\\frac{\\sqrt{558}}{\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{186}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9429803b-519b-508a-b3cc-201f034f107b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645396} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7df5621-bf5a-585c-af2a-87a2f41e3cfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef100ea7-7607-5952-94aa-26ff2e8f20a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316354" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $u=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ and $v=⟨-3,4,-2⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =2i+4j+5k \\\\  & =⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,u\\times v\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ca3dc8-3fe5-552f-90c4-e245abc46d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645153} {:answer/id #uuid "50b3411f-38cd-5ddf-a736-df1b86930387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645279} {:answer/id #uuid "51b2c834-ca61-5bae-a1d8-cdbae1f2990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645281} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc1b017-e397-5fbc-8e34-62f517b1904c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645380} {:answer/id #uuid "bd276bba-146e-527f-a4f0-aa840c1a49b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186645281} :answer-field/id #uuid "2667f05d-8a9f-5cc2-9f3c-ef42133f74fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186645589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f34ad469-37af-538b-92a9-b213d6d779f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-19986" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $c=⟨-1,2,1⟩$ and $d=⟨-1,0,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\times d & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 1)i-((-1)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1)j+((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =6i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,c\\times d\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f089e4-c72f-5629-8f74-36ec982c2488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653188} {:answer/id #uuid "7edba1b3-9322-5ee2-b69f-6a487d2fd95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653264} {:answer/id #uuid "802c1233-d6b6-53e0-beae-ce43f885ccd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653266} {:answer/id #uuid "c042a7fa-64ad-56b4-bf49-b735ac557f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653350} {:answer/id #uuid "dc74d1ad-6a4c-5a75-9b92-c2f766004a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653400}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653266} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f70265e-a03b-5c0d-ad9d-127cad624f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0448344-c055-5396-aa36-c0a21ea26403" :question/math-academy-id "q-125421" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7185759e7ed2211fa82e39b3731969a2593645a4da90ce46289b290ff4c32fd.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$ works with the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/30/306fdb7f7a6054d214940d1ca9614c35905f015f34d175561a7850781b751a93.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ea0397e7ba2a2ce6aef1058c6935a3282e12df5adc3b3d564cfb87c5f76c3b.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d13a9b-00d0-5062-a271-ca74477c41e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656391} {:answer/id #uuid "39035dcf-d063-53ef-9313-e80c580df45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656462} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe9b9ed-ff3d-5d8a-9714-8d8bb1bb4f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656496} {:answer/id #uuid "d16a63b5-5793-5938-a6e2-4fdf06b1905d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656693} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1b118d-6e1d-50df-ad36-8f01b87bf66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656391} :answer-field/id #uuid "035f06d7-3b6b-57df-bf28-f73a2366fd7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186656770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abb70047-6870-5e06-9fac-05c23b316ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161261" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-v,\\,3uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩|}_{x=2{u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=3uv} \\\\  & =⟨-(-v),\\,-3uv,\\,-(2{u}^{2})(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-3uv,\\,2{u}^{2}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v,\\,-3uv,\\,2{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩ \\\\  & =v\\cdot 3v+(-3uv)\\cdot (-12{u}^{2})+2{u}^{2}v\\cdot (-4u) \\\\  & =3{v}^{2}+36{u}^{3}v-8{u}^{3}v \\\\  & =3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{v}^{2}+28{u}^{3}v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{v}^{3}+14{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-(-1)+14{u}^{3}-14{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2[u]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c9ec0e-66d3-5cba-a831-965f770337f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658411} {:answer/id #uuid "9feeefe5-46a6-5f2c-be0e-1f355fe4c22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{105}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658636} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1cf622-c511-5224-9b9e-42a9d6776c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{210}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658712} {:answer/id #uuid "d046a6ea-67c9-5262-a7a7-d00cb79c23ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658718} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ba42d2-412a-59c6-b72b-e4885b44b903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{210}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658763} :answer-field/id #uuid "4353e836-7643-54dd-ac0d-6b6930d35791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186658843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b415c9d2-7008-5864-ac74-7937c5b3b310" :question/math-academy-id "q-162888" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=xz\\,j+k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =xz\\,j+k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k \\\\  & =(-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(4t-2)\\,j+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\,j+k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(4t-2)\\,j+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+(-{t}^{5}+2{t}^{4}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (4t-2)+1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{4}+10{t}^{5}-4{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{4}+10{t}^{5}-4{t}^{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{6}-\\frac{4}{7}{t}^{7}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{210}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa88715-8622-5064-b078-77c2672ab91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662944} {:answer/id #uuid "1da7e5ee-c042-5ae1-bb5e-fa65a251e246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662963} {:answer/id #uuid "8898bd9d-70ca-5173-b47d-6c8625602ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663101} {:answer/id #uuid "bd598838-d894-5fba-b7f7-8ad8bdf9cd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663183} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ec26a-f5fe-5a82-bbab-d79fdf335c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663223} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85a3c33-9e7f-51b2-8d1a-55b5c7d2f248" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "55a7865e-69f8-5071-8375-101634f071df" :question/math-academy-id "q-128022" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $VH$ of the $△\\,TUV,$ if $T(4,2,0),$ $U(1,3,2),$ $V(2,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =(i+3j+2k)-(4i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(2i+4j)-(4i+2j) \\\\  & =-2i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 2)j+((-3)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-4i-4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60945632-d0b0-52bc-bc00-671a7e9d7e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663048}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663048} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1f1d84-e989-5c2c-8e93-a6c17e98d24b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "702c328b-48dc-5cc2-aaf9-97fa8fcbba03" :question/math-academy-id "q-316550" :question/problem "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,GHI$, if $G(1,1,1)$, $H(3,2,1)$, $I(2,2,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+0k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+2j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GI} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GH}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186663409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36822a47-15ab-538e-b96c-d79e28a367b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{114}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662998} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4ff416-ef60-5836-a363-2b0ee8d47cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663026} {:answer/id #uuid "87b4c61a-63e8-50eb-8b99-b2bd5b0156ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663097} {:answer/id #uuid "cede378e-b315-5553-8515-4a35275a1d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663207} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c4facb-74d5-5896-8330-7d8f2333c1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662998} :answer-field/id #uuid "9392b129-550e-5c87-93da-c515d115bee2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186663322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e4c7314-f1bf-5eea-9885-487b8ee18a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127907" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(3,1,2),$ $B(6,5,6),$ $C(2,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(6i+5j+6k)-(3i+j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+4j+4k \\\\  & =⟨3,4,4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4)i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4)j+(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-8i-j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{114}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{114}}{2}.\n$$"}]}
